1          /*==============================================================================
2            TempleCBE SAS Validation Benchmark: PROC COMPARE against cbe_compare_df()
3          
4            Nine small comparisons, each on data typed into this program (so R and SAS see
5            the very same numbers; a test in test-sas_parity_reference.R checks the
6            datalines). All use METHOD=ABSOLUTE and a CRITERION that is the tolerance given
7            to cbe_compare_df(): 1E-7 (its default), except s4_edge (0.25) and s9_trap (0.1).
8          
9              s1_id       ID id. Identical variables, a difference inside and beyond the
10                         criterion, a missing value on one side only, a missing value on
11                         both sides, character differences (including case and a blank),
12                         a variable that is numeric in BASE and character in COMPARE, a
13                         variable only in BASE and one only in COMPARE, a character
14                         variable with different lengths, two observations only in BASE
15                         and one only in COMPARE. COMPARE is typed unsorted and sorted
16                         here, because PROC COMPARE needs sorted data; cbe_compare_df()
17                         does not.
18             s2_rows     No ID: observations are compared by position; BASE has two more.
19             s3_dup      ID values that occur more than once (paired in order).
20             s4_edge     Differences of exactly the criterion, just below and just above.
21             s5_decimal  Differences of 9E-8 and 1.1E-7 at criterion 1E-7, on numbers of
22                         different size and in both directions.
23             s6_same     A data set compared with a copy of itself.
24             s7_within   Differences that are all inside the criterion.
25             s8_text     Character differences: case, and a trailing blank.
26             s9_trap     A difference of 0.1 (as a decimal) at criterion 0.1: decimal
27                         fractions are not exact in binary, so it is a difference for
28                         1 against 1.1 and none for 0.5 against 0.6.
29         
30           The listing is read by tests/testthat/helper-sas-reference.R (parse_compare_lst);
31           the numbers are in tests/testthat/reference/sas_proc_compare.csv. SYSINFO is the
2                                           The SAS System               15:21 Monday, October 5, 2026

32           return code PROC COMPARE leaves in &SYSINFO (a bit mask), saved before the DATA
33           step that prints it, because a DATA step resets it.
34         
35           Can be executed directly from R:
36             TempleCBE::run_sas_script(
37               TempleCBE::cbe_sas_macro_path("benchmark_proc_compare.sas")
38             )
39         ==============================================================================*/
40         
41         options nodate nonumber linesize=100 pagesize=32767;
